Cyborg Pluto Nuclear Tight Security - the CPC edition of Cyborg NTS.

Keys and plaintext never leave the CPC; only what KEYMAN has encoded
goes on the wire (the TCP/IP stack is on the M4, a separate chip).

THE SIX LAYERS (a key each, any mix, 1 to 3 deep)
  z   ZOSCII          16-bit addresses into the key's bytes
  u   UNSIGNAL        z plus a random offset and prefix / suffix
  b   BRICS           u in blocks, each at its own offset
  mz  microZOSCII     8-bit addresses into the key's nibbles
  mu  microUNSIGNAL   mz with the offset and padding
  mb  microBRICS      mz in blocks
The micro three are for key exchange and are usually the inner layer.
A Pluto file is a plain UNSIGNAL / BRICS file: the PC NTS tools read
it, and it reads theirs.

KEYS
  Keys live in KEYS/NAME.KEY (the ROM's raw bytes). The size says the
  kind: 128 bytes a microROM, more a full ROM (e.g. 4K, 64K, 128K).
  Make one with KEYMGR:
    KEYMGR LIST                      the keys you have
    KEYMGR JPEG NAME pic.jpg 4096    a key from the first 4096 bytes
    KEYMGR ENTROPY NAME 128 f1 f2 f3 f4 f5   a key XORed from files,
                                     seeded by the clock, uptime and
                                     free memory (the ROM generator's way)

ENCODE / DECODE A FILE  (the layers left to right; DEC in the same order)
  ENC mu:inner u:mykey message.txt message.pcb
  DEC mu:inner u:mykey message.pcb back.txt
The outer u / b layer sizes its random suffix so the file is a whole
number of 128-byte records - the CPC's own floppy format needs no
padding of its own, and the suffix is discarded on decode.

